Quoting  RjY  <rjyATusersDOTsourceforgeDOTnet>:
>David Damerell posted:
>>If you really suppose vanilla NetHack is so enormously hard, then why
>>scorn rigid attention to detail? Not wasting wishes is surely helpful in
>>winning this supposedly enormously hard game.
>The original post was YA _F_ AP. Some imperfection is to be expected. To
>the uninitiated, the game's reputation _is_ that it is enormously hard.
>Thus, for most, a first ascension feels like an achievement, regardless
>of minor error.

I mostly don't disagree with this, but I don't see that it is particularly
relevant either.

I do disagree that squandering a wish is a minor error. You can often get
away with it, because vanilla gives you a lot of wishes, but to throw away
one of the most powerful tools in your armoury for a point of AC is hardly
a trivial mistake.

>In other words, to the first-time ascender, optimisation of the fine
>details after the fact is of little concern. Rigid attention to detail
>comes later.

I absolutely do disagree with this. To win at NetHack, as I see it,
demands four things; good strategy (for example, recognising once Rodney
is dead that your objective is not to kill every monster you see), good
tactics (in those parts of the game where you face a genuine threat),
knowledge of the game mechanics (whether by reading spoilers or deducing
facts, the Crawl devs hate it but we have to have it), and attention to
detail or self-discipline.

It's the last of those that never attacks a floating eye in melee without
reflection (and never messes up armour and amulets so as to be without
reflection and not know it), never steps near the water when drowning
might result, never wastes a potentially useful potion or scroll, never
takes a dangerous step wielding a cockatrice corpse ...

Now of course the attention to detail does not have to be perfect, because
vanilla NetHack is secretly not as hard as its reputation would suggest,
and sometimes luck will let you muddle through; and strength in any of
those four areas can cover up for weakness in another; but, all other
factors being equal, better attention to detail significantly increases
the odds of ascension, and indeed until you develop at least some
self-discipline you are vanishingly unlikely to ascend.

When you want a first ascension, you should seek ways to improve your play
- including attention to detail; if you've had one and would like another,
it's a pretty safe bet you can still find ways to improve your play -
including attention to detail - and where better to look than in the first
ascension report for mistakes that happened not to be fatal?

[This is a bit reminiscent of the mad decision to look at YAFAPs to find
out how to ascend for the first time. YAFAPs tend to be the worst APs for
advice because the author's play is likely only just to be good enough to
ascend, and the ascension report likely will contain mistakes which
happened not to be bad enough to be fatal.]

Obviously it's up to the OP if they want to recognise that they made a
mistake and resolve not to make it again, or to get their knickers in a
twist; but I don't think we should stop offering good advice merely
because some of it will fall on stony ground.
